<!--
  The Windows installer: installs den-watcher.exe as the Windows service
  "Den-Watcher", which starts with Windows, and opens its port in the
  Windows firewall for private networks only. By default the service only
  serves this PC's usage to a hub (DATA_ONLY); with WEBSITE=1 it also shows
  the website and keeps the history itself. Double-clicking the installer
  asks which of the two this PC should do (the "Mode" page below); a newer
  version's installer asks again, and a repair of the installed version
  switches with the option (msiexec /i den-watcher.msi REINSTALL=ALL
  REINSTALLMODE=m WEBSITE=1).

  Add-ons are optional programs that track more, each writing to
  C:\ProgramData\Usage Control\addons, which den-watcher shows. The service
  starts the installed add-ons through the add-on host, a den-watcher.exe of
  its own, so Task Manager groups them under it, except the smart add-on,
  which needs more rights and is a service of its own. The Edition page
  picks Cub, the lightweight add-ons, or Grizzly, whose page offers what to
  track in groups (System details, Hardware, Network, Bluetooth,
  Processes), each add-on as a box below its group, and each has an install
  option. POWER=1 installs the power add-on,
  den-watcher-power.exe, which reads the power the energy meters Windows
  offers report (CPU, memory and laptop meters), what the battery gives
  while the PC runs on it, and what NVIDIA GPUs draw.
  GPU=1 installs the gpu add-on, den-watcher-gpu.exe, which reads the fan,
  clocks and more of NVIDIA GPUs.
  PRESSURE=1 installs the pressure add-on, den-watcher-pressure.exe, which
  reads how many threads wait for a processor, how many pages are read from
  disk for memory and how busy the disks are.
  KERNEL=1 installs the kernel add-on, den-watcher-kernel.exe, which reads
  context switches, interrupts, handles, TCP connections and
  retransmissions.
  WIFI=1 installs the Wi-Fi add-on, den-watcher-wifi.exe, which reads the
  signal of each connected Wi-Fi adapter and, unless WIFI_NEARBY=0, the
  networks nearby, asking Windows to scan a connected adapter every 5
  minutes unless WIFI_SCAN=0.
  MEMORY=1 installs the memory add-on, den-watcher-memory.exe, which reads
  the Memory performance counters.
  PORTS=1 installs the ports add-on, den-watcher-ports.exe, which lists the
  ports the PC listens on.
  SMART=1 installs the smart add-on, den-watcher-smart.exe, as the
  service "Den-Watcher smart add-on", which reads the disks' health; it
  runs as LocalSystem, as only administrators may send disks SMART commands.
  PROCESSES=1 installs the processes add-on, den-watcher-processes.exe,
  which lists the processes using the most CPU and memory.
  BLUETOOTH=1 installs the Bluetooth add-on, den-watcher-bluetooth.exe,
  which lists the connected classic Bluetooth devices, such as headsets and
  speakers, by their names unless BLUETOOTH_NAMES=0.

  It also installs den-watcher-tray.exe, the icon in the taskbar that shows
  whether the service runs and pauses, stops or opens it. It starts when
  someone logs in, and at the end of the setup wizard, also after a repair
  from the wizard, which closes it. Logged-in users may start and stop the
  service, so its menu needs no administrator.

  Built by .github/workflows/windows.yml with WiX Toolset 5:
    wix build windows/den-watcher.wxs -arch x64 -d Version=1.2.3 -d Bugfix=0 -d Binary=den-watcher.exe
      -d TrayBinary=den-watcher-tray.exe -d AddOns=. -d Icon=den-watcher.ico
      -ext WixToolset.Firewall.wixext -ext WixToolset.Util.wixext -ext WixToolset.UI.wixext
      -o den-watcher.msi

  Version is the installer's version; a bugfix release such as 1.2.3a puts
  its letters in the fourth number (1.2.3.1), and Bugfix is that number, 0
  for none.

  Install options (msiexec /i den-watcher.msi PORT=8090 WEBSITE=1):
    PORT            port the PC is reachable on (default 9393)
    WEBSITE         1 to show the website and keep the history on this PC,
                    0 to only serve the usage to a hub (default 0)
    DEVICE_NAME     how this PC names itself: on its page with WEBSITE=1, and
                    as the name a hub offers when this PC is added from its
                    browser (default: the page says Host Hub, the hub offers
                    the PC's name)
    HUB_DEVICES     with WEBSITE=1, other devices to collect from (hub mode)
    HUB_ROUTERS     with WEBSITE=1, routers to show as devices, each as
                    name=protocol:address, such as Router=upnp:192.168.1.1 or
                    Box=fritzbox:monitor@192.168.178.1 (see docs/setup.md)
    HUB_CHECKS      with WEBSITE=1, things that run no den-watcher, such as
                    a printer, to show as devices the hub only checks for
                    whether they answer, each as name=address:port to connect
                    to a TCP port or name=address to ping, such as
                    Printer=192.168.1.50:631 or NAS=192.168.1.10
    ROUTER_PASSWORDS  the passwords of those routers that need a login, and
                    SNMP communities other than public, as name=password; not
                    remembered: the service encrypts them with DPAPI into
                    router-passwords.dpapi in its data folder at its next
                    start and deletes them from the registry. Routers it
                    already has a password for keep it without this, until
                    uninstalling deletes them
  Uninstall options (msiexec /x den-watcher.msi DELETE_HISTORY=1), which the
  Remove page of the installer asks for, each with a box of its own:
    DELETE_HISTORY  1 to also delete the history (default: kept, so
                    reinstalling keeps it)
    DELETE_ROUTER_PASSWORDS  0 to keep the encrypted router passwords
                    (default 1: deleted)
    RETENTION_DAYS  with WEBSITE=1, days of history to keep (default 30)
    HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES  with WEBSITE=1, how many disks, sensors, network cards
                    and GPUs each the history keeps per device (default 64)
    BUFFER_HOURS    without WEBSITE=1, hours of usage kept for a hub that
                    cannot reach this PC, from 1 to 168 (default 24)
    ALLOWED_HOSTS   other names this PC answers to, besides its IP addresses,
                    localhost, its hostname and .local names; comma-separated
    DISK_PATHS      drives or folders whose disks are shown, comma-separated
                    (default: the system drive); the service does not start
                    while one cannot be read, and says which in the event log
    UPDATE_CHECK    with WEBSITE=1, false to stop the daily check for a newer
                    release (default true); the installer refuses a value
                    the service cannot read: it takes true, false, 1, 0, t
                    and f, in the cases the service takes, without spaces
                    around. To fix a bad remembered value of the installed
                    version, repair it with its own installer:
                      msiexec /i den-watcher.msi REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m UPDATE_CHECK=true
    RESET_PASSWORD  with WEBSITE=1, true to delete the password for changing
                    devices when the service starts; only from an
                    administrator, and not remembered, so the next install,
                    update or repair without it turns it off. To use it with
                    the installed version, repair it with its own installer:
                      msiexec /i den-watcher.msi REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m RESET_PASSWORD=true
                    and then once more the same way without RESET_PASSWORD
    POWER           1 to install the power add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
                    (default: not installed)
    GPU             1 to install the gpu add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    PRESSURE        1 to install the pressure add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    KERNEL          1 to install the kernel add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    WIFI            1 to install the Wi-Fi add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    WIFI_SCAN       with WIFI=1, 0 to stop asking Windows to scan a connected
                    Wi-Fi adapter for the networks nearby every 5 minutes,
                    as each scan can delay the connection for a moment
                    (default 1)
    WIFI_NEARBY     with WIFI=1, 0 to read only the connected adapters'
                    quality and signal, not the networks nearby, which are
                    then neither read nor scanned for (default 1)
    MEMORY          1 to install the memory add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    PORTS           1 to install the ports add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    SMART           1 to install the smart add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    BLUETOOTH       1 to install the Bluetooth add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    BLUETOOTH_NAMES with BLUETOOTH=1, 0 to show each device by its kind, such
                    as Headphones, instead of its name (default 1)
    PROCESSES       1 to install the processes add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
                    (default: not installed); the installer refuses any
                    other value for these
  An update keeps the options of the installed version, except RESET_PASSWORD;
  options given to the update replace them. An option given empty counts as
  not given, so to clear a remembered PORT, DEVICE_NAME, HUB_DEVICES,
  HUB_ROUTERS, HUB_CHECKS, RETENTION_DAYS, HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES, BUFFER_HOURS, ALLOWED_HOSTS,
  DISK_PATHS, UPDATE_CHECK, WIFI_SCAN, WIFI_NEARBY or BLUETOOTH_NAMES, give it a single space, such as DISK_PATHS=" ",
  which means its default. WEBSITE and the add-ons take 0 to turn them off.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:fw="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/firewall"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">
  <Package Name="Den-Watcher"
           Manufacturer="den-watcher"
           Version="$(Version)"
           UpgradeCode="C226A238-37CF-4C24-85FE-A29D04C94172"
           Scope="perMachine">
    <!-- Installing a newer version replaces the old one and keeps the history.
         A bugfix release only changes the fourth number of the version, which
         Windows Installer ignores, so the same version counts as an update too. -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
                  DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of Den-Watcher is already installed." />
    <!--
      For the same reason an older bugfix of the same version would replace a
      newer one, so each install remembers its bugfix number (Bugfix), and an
      installer of the same version (SAMEVERSION, which compares only the
      first three numbers) with a lower one refuses to install.
    -->
    <Upgrade Id="C226A238-37CF-4C24-85FE-A29D04C94172">
      <UpgradeVersion Minimum="$(Version)" Maximum="$(Version)" IncludeMinimum="yes" IncludeMaximum="yes"
                      OnlyDetect="yes" Property="SAMEVERSION" />
    </Upgrade>
    <Property Id="INSTALLEDBUGFIX">
      <RegistrySearch Id="RememberedBugfix" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="Bugfix" Type="raw" />
    </Property>
    <Launch Condition="Installed OR NOT SAMEVERSION OR NOT INSTALLEDBUGFIX OR INSTALLEDBUGFIX &lt;= $(Bugfix)"
            Message="A newer bugfix of this version of Den-Watcher is already installed." />
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />
    <!-- The mascot, in Apps and Features. -->
    <Icon Id="UsageControl.ico" SourceFile="$(Icon)" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="UsageControl.ico" />

    <!--
      Each option is remembered in the registry. AppSearch reads the remembered
      value, which would overwrite one given on the command line, so that one
      is saved before and put back after, in both the UI and the install.
    -->
    <?foreach Setting in PORT;WEBSITE;DEVICE_NAME;HUB_DEVICES;HUB_ROUTERS;HUB_CHECKS;RETENTION_DAYS;HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES;BUFFER_HOURS;ALLOWED_HOSTS;DISK_PATHS;UPDATE_CHECK;POWER;GPU;PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;WIFI_SCAN;WIFI_NEARBY;MEMORY;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES;BLUETOOTH;BLUETOOTH_NAMES ?>
    <Property Id="$(Setting)" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="Remembered$(Setting)" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="$(Setting)" Type="raw" />
    </Property>
    <SetProperty Action="Save$(Setting)" Id="CMDLINE_$(Setting)" Value="[$(Setting)]" Before="AppSearch" Sequence="both" Condition="$(Setting)" />
    <SetProperty Action="Restore$(Setting)" Id="$(Setting)" Value="[CMDLINE_$(Setting)]" After="AppSearch" Sequence="both" Condition="CMDLINE_$(Setting)" />
    <?endforeach?>
    <!--
      RESET_PASSWORD is the one option that is not remembered: while it is set,
      every start of the service deletes the password, so it applies only to
      the install, update or repair it is given to. A standard user may start
      a repair of an installed program too, so the service gets it only from
      an administrator (ServiceResetPassword); MSIUSEREALADMINDETECTION makes
      AdminUser tell a real administrator, not only an elevated install.
    -->
    <Property Id="RESET_PASSWORD" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="MSIUSEREALADMINDETECTION" Value="1" />
    <SetProperty Action="ResetPasswordByAdmin" Id="ServiceResetPassword" Value="[RESET_PASSWORD]" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition="AdminUser AND RESET_PASSWORD" />

    <!--
      The service refuses to start with an UPDATE_CHECK it cannot read, and
      would then fail again and again, so the installer refuses it instead. It
      takes what the service takes, as Go's strconv.ParseBool reads it, except
      spaces around the value, which the service trims but a condition cannot;
      a single space means the default. The launch conditions run after the
      remembered options are read, so this also checks those, in a repair too,
      and names the option that fixes a bad remembered value. For the version
      that is installed, msiexec /i with only the option does nothing, so the
      command it names then repairs it with the option (UpdateCheckFix). Only
      an uninstall (msiexec /x, as from Apps and Features, which sets REMOVE)
      is not held up by it.
    -->
    <Property Id="UpdateCheckFix" Value="UPDATE_CHECK=true" />
    <SetProperty Action="UpdateCheckFixInstalled" Id="UpdateCheckFix" Value="REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m UPDATE_CHECK=true" Before="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition="Installed" />
    <Launch Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" OR NOT UPDATE_CHECK OR UPDATE_CHECK = " " OR UPDATE_CHECK = "true" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "false" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "True" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "False" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "TRUE" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "FALSE" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "t" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "f" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "T" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "F" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "1" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "0"'
            Message='UPDATE_CHECK must be true or false. To give it, run the installer from a command prompt run as administrator: msiexec /i "[OriginalDatabase]" [UpdateCheckFix]' />

    <!--
      The settings that choose what an add-on collects (WIFI_SCAN and
      WIFI_NEARBY for the Wi-Fi add-on, BLUETOOTH_NAMES for the Bluetooth
      add-on): the add-on only warns about a value other than 0 or 1 and
      takes 1, so the installer refuses one as it does a bad UPDATE_CHECK,
      rather than let a mistyped 0 go unnoticed; a single space means the
      default.
    -->
    <?foreach Switch in WIFI_SCAN;WIFI_NEARBY;BLUETOOTH_NAMES ?>
    <Property Id="$(Switch)Fix" Value="$(Switch)=1" />
    <SetProperty Action="$(Switch)FixInstalled" Id="$(Switch)Fix" Value="REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m $(Switch)=1" Before="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition="Installed" />
    <Launch Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" OR NOT $(Switch) OR $(Switch) = " " OR $(Switch) = "0" OR $(Switch) = "1"'
            Message='$(Switch) must be 0 or 1. To give it, run the installer from a command prompt run as administrator: msiexec /i "[OriginalDatabase]" [$(Switch)Fix]' />
    <!-- Its box on the Grizzly page, ticked unless it is 0. -->
    <SetProperty Action="Box$(Switch)" Id="$(Switch)_BOX" Value="1" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui" Condition='$(Switch) &lt;&gt; "0"' />
    <?endforeach?>

    <!--
      Derived from the options, once all of them are known. Without WEBSITE=1
      the service only serves the usage to a hub, and ignores HUB_DEVICES,
      HUB_ROUTERS and HUB_CHECKS. DataOnly, ServiceHubDevices, ServiceHubRouters and ServiceHubChecks are private (mixed case), so the setup
      wizard does not pass them on to the install: they are worked out there
      from WEBSITE as the wizard left it, also when the wizard switched it
      from the remembered 1 to 0.
    -->
    <SetProperty Action="DefaultPORT" Id="PORT" Value="9393" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition='NOT PORT OR PORT = " "' />
    <SetProperty Action="DefaultWEBSITE" Id="WEBSITE" Value="0" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition="NOT WEBSITE" />
    <Property Id="DataOnly" Value="true" />
    <SetProperty Action="WebsiteOn" Id="DataOnly" Value="false" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Action="WebsiteHubDevices" Id="ServiceHubDevices" Value="[HUB_DEVICES]" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Action="WebsiteHubRouters" Id="ServiceHubRouters" Value="[HUB_ROUTERS]" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Action="WebsiteHubChecks" Id="ServiceHubChecks" Value="[HUB_CHECKS]" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />

    <!--
      The router passwords are not remembered with the other options: the
      installer writes them to a registry key that only the system,
      administrators and the service's account can read, and the service
      moves them from there into a file in its data folder, encrypted with
      DPAPI, and deletes them from the registry. Windows Installer would log
      a value it writes to the registry itself, even of a hidden property,
      so reg.exe writes them, in a custom action whose command line is
      hidden. As a multi-string ending in \0, a password may end in a
      backslash; reg.exe cannot take a double quote in it, which the Router
      page refuses, as it refuses the two characters \0, which would split
      the value. Hidden keeps them out of the installer's log otherwise. It
      runs once the key's permissions are set (they are set after
      InstallServices), so the password is never in a key others can read.
      ROUTER_SECRET is the password field on the Router page (see RouterDlg).
    -->
    <Property Id="ROUTER_PASSWORDS" Secure="yes" Hidden="yes" />
    <Property Id="ROUTER_SECRET" Hidden="yes" />
    <Property Id="StoreRouterPasswords" Hidden="yes" />
    <Property Id="Quote" Value='"' />
    <SetProperty Id="StoreRouterPasswords" Before="StoreRouterPasswords" Sequence="execute"
                 Value='"[System64Folder]reg.exe" add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Usage Control\Router" /v Passwords /t REG_MULTI_SZ /d "[ROUTER_PASSWORDS]\0" /f /reg:64'
                 Condition='ROUTER_PASSWORDS AND WEBSITE = "1" AND REMOVE &lt;&gt; "ALL"' />
    <CustomAction Id="StoreRouterPasswords" BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_$(sys.BUILDARCHSHORT)" DllEntry="WixSilentExec"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" HideTarget="yes" Return="check" />
    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="StoreRouterPasswords" Before="StartServices" Condition='ROUTER_PASSWORDS AND WEBSITE = "1" AND REMOVE &lt;&gt; "ALL"' />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <!--
      Uninstalling deletes from the data folder, once the services are
      stopped, the add-on reports, the encrypted router passwords unless
      DELETE_ROUTER_PASSWORDS=0, and the history with DELETE_HISTORY=1: the
      two boxes on the Remove page (see RemoveDataDlg). With both, the folder
      is left empty and goes too. An update also uninstalls the version
      before it (UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE), which keeps them all, so an empty
      password in an update still keeps the one stored for that name. The
      path is spelled out from CommonAppDataFolder rather than DATAFOLDER,
      which a command line could point elsewhere.
    -->
    <Property Id="DELETE_HISTORY" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="DELETE_ROUTER_PASSWORDS" Value="1" Secure="yes" />
    <?foreach Data in AddOnReports;RouterPasswords;History ?>
    <CustomAction Id="Delete$(Data)" BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_$(sys.BUILDARCHSHORT)" DllEntry="WixQuietExec"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="ignore" />
    <?endforeach?>
    <SetProperty Id="DeleteAddOnReports" Before="DeleteAddOnReports" Sequence="execute"
                 Value='"[System64Folder]cmd.exe" /d /c del /f /q "[CommonAppDataFolder]Usage Control\addons\*.json"'
                 Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE' />
    <SetProperty Id="DeleteRouterPasswords" Before="DeleteRouterPasswords" Sequence="execute"
                 Value='"[System64Folder]cmd.exe" /d /c del /f /q "[CommonAppDataFolder]Usage Control\router-passwords.dpapi"'
                 Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE AND DELETE_ROUTER_PASSWORDS = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Id="DeleteHistory" Before="DeleteHistory" Sequence="execute"
                 Value='"[System64Folder]cmd.exe" /d /c del /f /q "[CommonAppDataFolder]Usage Control\usage-control.db*"'
                 Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE AND DELETE_HISTORY = "1"' />
    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="DeleteAddOnReports" After="StopServices" Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE' />
      <Custom Action="DeleteRouterPasswords" After="DeleteAddOnReports" Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE AND DELETE_ROUTER_PASSWORDS = "1"' />
      <Custom Action="DeleteHistory" After="DeleteRouterPasswords" Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE AND DELETE_HISTORY = "1"' />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <!--
      Each add-on's box on the Grizzly page has a property of its own, ticked
      when the add-on is on; Cub's add-ons are also ticked where nothing was
      given or remembered for them, so Grizzly starts from Cub (see
      CubBoxes). A cleared box leaves its property empty, which
      would let AppSearch bring back the remembered 1, so Next turns the box
      into 1 or 0 for the add-on's option (see AddOnsDlg).

      Any other value than 1 counts as off, and as an update or repair checks
      the add-on again (see the power add-on), a typo such as POWER=true would
      remove it. So, like UPDATE_CHECK, each add-on option takes only 0 or 1,
      also a remembered one, and the refusal names the command that gives it
      ($(AddOn)Fix, a repair for the installed version). An uninstall is not
      held up by it.
    -->
    <?foreach AddOn in POWER;GPU;PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;MEMORY;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES;BLUETOOTH ?>
    <SetProperty Action="Box$(AddOn)" Id="$(AddOn)_BOX" Value="1" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui" Condition='$(AddOn) = "1"' />
    <Property Id="$(AddOn)Fix" Value="$(AddOn)=1" />
    <SetProperty Action="$(AddOn)FixInstalled" Id="$(AddOn)Fix" Value="REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m $(AddOn)=1" Before="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition="Installed" />
    <Launch Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" OR NOT $(AddOn) OR $(AddOn) = "0" OR $(AddOn) = "1"'
            Message='$(AddOn) must be 0 or 1. To give it, run the installer from a command prompt run as administrator: msiexec /i "[OriginalDatabase]" [$(AddOn)Fix], or with $(AddOn)=0 to remove the add-on' />
    <!-- The remembered value once more, kept as it is, to tell whether a repair changes the add-on (AddOnFiles). -->
    <Property Id="REMEMBERED_$(AddOn)">
      <RegistrySearch Id="RememberedAgain$(AddOn)" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="$(AddOn)" Type="raw" />
    </Property>
    <?endforeach?>

    <?foreach AddOn in PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;MEMORY ?>
    <SetProperty Action="CubBox$(AddOn)" Id="$(AddOn)_BOX" Value="1" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui" Condition="NOT $(AddOn)" />
    <?endforeach?>

    <!--
      The groups on the Grizzly page, the same as install.sh's, Docker's
      profiles and the Android app's: System details (kernel, memory,
      pressure), Hardware (power, gpu, smart), Network (Wi-Fi, ports),
      Bluetooth and Processes. A group's box is ticked when one of its
      add-ons is, as its add-ons' boxes are worked out above; the groups of
      one add-on, Bluetooth and Processes, are that add-on's box.
    -->
    <SetProperty Action="GroupDetails" Id="DETAILS_BOX" Value="1" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui"
                 Condition='NOT KERNEL OR KERNEL = "1" OR NOT MEMORY OR MEMORY = "1" OR NOT PRESSURE OR PRESSURE = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Action="GroupHardware" Id="HARDWARE_BOX" Value="1" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui"
                 Condition='POWER = "1" OR GPU = "1" OR SMART = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Action="GroupNetwork" Id="NETWORK_BOX" Value="1" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui"
                 Condition='NOT WIFI OR WIFI = "1" OR PORTS = "1"' />

    <!--
      A repair with REINSTALLMODE=m copies and deletes no file, also not the
      program of an add-on it adds or removes. So a repair that changes an
      add-on option from the remembered one, with no letter in REINSTALLMODE
      for the files, also reinstalls missing or older files (o); the other
      files are the same, so it copies none of them. It sets this before
      costing, which reads it. It compares the values rather than asking
      whether the option was given, as with a user interface the remembered
      options reach the install as if given; a repair that gives the
      remembered value, or one without the option, keeps REINSTALLMODE=m and
      so the tray icons. Where nothing is remembered, as after an install
      before the add-on existed, an option given as 1 or 0 counts as a change.
    -->
    <SetProperty Action="AddOnFiles" Id="REINSTALLMODE" Value="omus" Before="CostInitialize" Sequence="execute"
                 Condition='REINSTALL AND NOT (REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "p" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "o" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "e" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "d" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "c" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "a") AND (POWER &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_POWER OR GPU &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_GPU OR PRESSURE &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_PRESSURE OR KERNEL &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_KERNEL OR WIFI &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_WIFI OR MEMORY &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_MEMORY OR PORTS &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_PORTS OR SMART &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_SMART OR PROCESSES &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_PROCESSES OR BLUETOOTH &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_BLUETOOTH)' />

    <!--
      The edition on the Edition page: Cub installs the lightweight add-ons
      (kernel, memory, pressure, Wi-Fi) and asks nothing more, Grizzly the
      add-ons and what they collect as picked group by group on the Grizzly
      page, which starts from Cub's. Not remembered itself: an update starts
      at Grizzly when one of its add-ons is installed, or one of Cub's is not.
    -->
    <Property Id="EDITION" Value="cub" />
    <SetProperty Action="EditionGrizzly" Id="EDITION" Value="grizzly" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui"
                 Condition='POWER = "1" OR GPU = "1" OR PROCESSES = "1" OR PORTS = "1" OR SMART = "1" OR BLUETOOTH = "1" OR KERNEL = "0" OR MEMORY = "0" OR PRESSURE = "0" OR WIFI = "0"' />

    <!--
      The router on the Router page, shown with the website on (hub mode):
      ROUTER_KIND is the way the hub reads it, and Next turns the page into
      HUB_ROUTERS and ROUTER_PASSWORDS. An update starts at keeping the
      routers it has.
    -->
    <Property Id="ROUTER_KIND" Value="none" />
    <Property Id="ROUTER_NAME" Value="Router" />
    <SetProperty Action="RouterKeep" Id="ROUTER_KIND" Value="keep" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui"
                 Condition='HUB_ROUTERS AND HUB_ROUTERS &lt;&gt; " "' />

    <!-- The setup wizard: welcome, the Mode page, the Router page with the website on, the Edition page, the Add-ons page for Grizzly, ready to install. -->
    <UIRef Id="SetupWizard" />
    <!-- No "Change" in Apps and Features: a newer installer, or a repair with WEBSITE, changes the mode. -->
    <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY" Value="1" />
    <!--
      Without the Restart Manager the installer only asks about programs with
      a window that hold its files. The add-ons the service runs have none,
      and stopping the service ends them before any file is changed, but the
      Restart Manager would count them as programs to close and, with a user
      interface, stop the install on a dialog asking about them.
    -->
    <Property Id="MSIRESTARTMANAGERCONTROL" Value="Disable" />

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Den-Watcher" />
    </StandardDirectory>
    <StandardDirectory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="DATAFOLDER" Name="Usage Control">
        <!-- Where add-ons write what they read, for the service to show. -->
        <Directory Id="ADDONSFOLDER" Name="addons" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <File Source="$(Binary)" Name="den-watcher.exe" KeyPath="yes" />

      <!-- A low-privilege account: reading the usage needs no administrator rights. -->
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControl"
                      DisplayName="Den-Watcher"
                      Description="Serves the usage of this computer on the local network."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <!--
          Windows' default rights for services, except that logged-in users
          (IU) may also start (RP) and stop (WP) this one, from the tray icon.
        -->
        <PermissionEx Sddl="D:(A;;CCLCSWRPWPDTLOCRRC;;;SY)(A;;CCDCLCSWRPWPDTLOCRSDRCWDWO;;;BA)(A;;CCLCSWRPWPLOCRRC;;;IU)(A;;CCLCSWLOCRRC;;;SU)" />
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControl" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />

      <!-- The service's settings, read by the program as environment variables. -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControl" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="LISTEN_ADDR=:[PORT]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DATA_ONLY=[DataOnly]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DATABASE_PATH=[DATAFOLDER]usage-control.db" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DEVICE_NAME=[DEVICE_NAME]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="HUB_DEVICES=[ServiceHubDevices]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="HUB_ROUTERS=[ServiceHubRouters]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="HUB_CHECKS=[ServiceHubChecks]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="RETENTION_DAYS=[RETENTION_DAYS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES=[HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="BUFFER_HOURS=[BUFFER_HOURS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="ALLOWED_HOSTS=[ALLOWED_HOSTS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DISK_PATHS=[DISK_PATHS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="UPDATE_CHECK=[UPDATE_CHECK]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="WIFI_SCAN=[WIFI_SCAN]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="WIFI_NEARBY=[WIFI_NEARBY]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="BLUETOOTH_NAMES=[BLUETOOTH_NAMES]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="RESET_PASSWORD=[ServiceResetPassword]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>

      <!--
        Where StoreRouterPasswords hands the router passwords to the service:
        only the system, administrators and the service's account can read
        the key, and the service may also delete the value in it. It is
        locked down as it is created.
      -->
      <RegistryKey Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control\Router" ForceCreateOnInstall="yes" ForceDeleteOnUninstall="yes">
        <PermissionEx Sddl="D:P(A;;KA;;;SY)(A;;KA;;;BA)(A;;0x2001b;;;LS)" />
      </RegistryKey>

      <!-- The options, read back by the next update, and the bugfix number (see Upgrade). -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="Bugfix" Type="string" Value="$(Bugfix)" />
      <?foreach Setting in PORT;WEBSITE;DEVICE_NAME;HUB_DEVICES;HUB_ROUTERS;HUB_CHECKS;RETENTION_DAYS;HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES;BUFFER_HOURS;ALLOWED_HOSTS;DISK_PATHS;UPDATE_CHECK;POWER;GPU;PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;WIFI_SCAN;WIFI_NEARBY;MEMORY;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES;BLUETOOTH;BLUETOOTH_NAMES ?>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="$(Setting)" Type="string" Value="[$(Setting)]" />
      <?endforeach?>

      <!-- Lets errors the service writes to the Application event log show as plain text. -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControl"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControl"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />

      <!-- The program itself also refuses requests from outside the local network. -->
      <fw:FirewallException Name="Den-Watcher" Port="[PORT]" Protocol="tcp" Profile="private" />
    </Component>

    <!-- The tray icon, started for everyone who logs in. -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <File Id="TrayExe" Source="$(TrayBinary)" Name="den-watcher-tray.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" Name="Den-Watcher"
                     Type="string" Value="&quot;[#TrayExe]&quot;" />
    </Component>
    <!--
      Updating and uninstalling close the tray icons that run, so the file can
      be replaced. A repair that only rewrites the registry (REINSTALLMODE=m,
      as for RESET_PASSWORD) replaces no file, so it leaves them running;
      one that changes an add-on checks the files too (AddOnFiles), so it
      closes them.
    -->
    <util:CloseApplication Target="den-watcher-tray.exe" CloseMessage="yes" ElevatedCloseMessage="yes" RebootPrompt="no"
                           Condition='NOT (REINSTALL AND REINSTALLMODE = "m")' />
    <!-- The tray icon from before the rename, which an update from usage-control closes. -->
    <util:CloseApplication Target="usage-control-tray.exe" CloseMessage="yes" ElevatedCloseMessage="yes" RebootPrompt="no"
                           Condition='NOT (REINSTALL AND REINSTALLMODE = "m")' />

    <!--
      The power add-on, with POWER=1: a program the Den-Watcher service
      starts, through its add-on host, when it finds it next to itself, so it
      runs in the service's low-privilege account, shows under it in Task
      Manager and stops with it (see addons.StartHost); it writes to the
      add-on folder, and its warnings to the event log under its own name.
      An install from before it was a child had it as a service of its own,
      which the update removes with the old version. An update
      checks the condition again, so it also adds or removes the add-on.
      Each add-on is transitive, so a repair checks it again too (REINSTALL=ALL
      REINSTALLMODE=m POWER=1 adds it, POWER=0 removes it). A repair without
      the option reads the remembered one, which the service's component
      writes back, so it keeps the add-ons as they are. A repair that only
      rewrites the registry (REINSTALLMODE=m) would copy or delete no file,
      so a repair that changes an add-on also checks the files (AddOnFiles).
      The repair stops the service before it changes the files and starts it
      after, which then starts the add-ons it finds.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='POWER = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-power.exe" Name="den-watcher-power.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPower"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPower"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The gpu add-on, with GPU=1, like the power add-on.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='GPU = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-gpu.exe" Name="den-watcher-gpu.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlGPU"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlGPU"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The pressure add-on, with PRESSURE=1, like the power add-on. Any account
      may read the performance counters it reads.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='PRESSURE = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-pressure.exe" Name="den-watcher-pressure.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPressure"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPressure"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The kernel add-on, with KERNEL=1, like the power add-on. Its
      performance counters, GetPerformanceInfo and GetTcpStatisticsEx need
      no more than the Local Service account.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='KERNEL = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-kernel.exe" Name="den-watcher-kernel.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlKernel"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlKernel"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The Wi-Fi add-on, with WIFI=1, in the same low-privilege account: the
      Native Wifi API lets any account read the signal of the connections.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='WIFI = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-wifi.exe" Name="den-watcher-wifi.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlWifi"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlWifi"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!-- The memory add-on, with MEMORY=1, set up like the power add-on. -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='MEMORY = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-memory.exe" Name="den-watcher-memory.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlMemory"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlMemory"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!-- The ports add-on, with PORTS=1, like the power add-on. -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='PORTS = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-ports.exe" Name="den-watcher-ports.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPorts"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPorts"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The smart add-on, with SMART=1: reads the disks' health with the SMART
      commands of the disk drivers (SATA) and the NVMe driver's health log.
      Windows lets only administrators and LocalSystem open a disk for the
      read and write access these commands need, so this service runs as
      LocalSystem, unlike the others. It sends only commands that read.
      Before reading a disk it asks Windows whether it has switched the disk
      off, and a SATA disk also whether it sleeps, and leaves it asleep.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='SMART = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-smart.exe" Name="den-watcher-smart.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlSmart"
                      DisplayName="Den-Watcher smart add-on"
                      Description="Reads the health of this computer's disks for Den-Watcher."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="LocalSystem">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlSmart" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlSmart" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlSmart"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlSmart"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The processes add-on, with PROCESSES=1, like the power add-on. Local
      Service sees every process: it asks Windows for all of them in one call
      instead of opening each one.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='PROCESSES = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-processes.exe" Name="den-watcher-processes.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlProcesses"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlProcesses"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The Bluetooth add-on, with BLUETOOTH=1, like the power add-on. Windows'
      Bluetooth API lets any account list the paired devices and whether they
      are connected; it only lists, and never searches for devices.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='BLUETOOTH = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\den-watcher-bluetooth.exe" Name="den-watcher-bluetooth.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlBluetooth"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlBluetooth"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The database: the history with WEBSITE=1, otherwise the minutes kept for
      a hub that cannot reach this PC (BUFFER_HOURS). Only the service,
      administrators and the system can read or change it. Uninstalling keeps
      it, so reinstalling keeps the history, unless DELETE_HISTORY=1 (see
      DeleteHistory).
    -->
    <Component Id="DataFolder" Directory="DATAFOLDER" Guid="C3AF9720-FF5B-4D29-AA9B-A3FB303E3586">
      <CreateFolder>
        <PermissionEx Sddl="D:PAI(A;OICI;FA;;;SY)(A;OICI;FA;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;LS)" />
      </CreateFolder>
    </Component>
    <!-- The add-on folder, with the data folder's rights. -->
    <Component Id="AddOnsFolder" Directory="ADDONSFOLDER" Guid="5E0F3C1B-7D52-4E4B-9C8E-2A6F1D93B4C7">
      <CreateFolder />
    </Component>
  </Package>

  <!--
    The wizard is WiX's minimal one with the Mode page added after the
    welcome page. Silent installs (msiexec /qn) skip it and use WEBSITE.
  -->
  <Fragment>
    <UI Id="SetupWizard">
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Normal" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="8" />
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Bigger" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="12" />
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Title" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="9" Bold="yes" />
      <Property Id="DefaultUIFont" Value="WixUI_Font_Normal" />

      <DialogRef Id="ErrorDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="FatalError" />
      <DialogRef Id="FilesInUse" />
      <DialogRef Id="MsiRMFilesInUse" />
      <DialogRef Id="PrepareDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ProgressDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ResumeDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="UserExit" />

      <Dialog Id="ModeDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}What should this PC do?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="280" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Installing a newer version later can change it." />
        <Control Id="Mode" Type="RadioButtonGroup" X="20" Y="60" Width="330" Height="110" Property="WEBSITE">
          <RadioButtonGroup Property="WEBSITE">
            <RadioButton Value="0" X="0" Y="0" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Only collect this PC's usage for a hub (recommended)" />
            <RadioButton Value="1" X="0" Y="45" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Collect and also show the website on this PC" />
          </RadioButtonGroup>
        </Control>
        <Control Id="CollectorText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="77" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Another device, such as a Raspberry Pi, shows this PC and keeps its history. Nothing else runs here." />
        <Control Id="WebsiteText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="122" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="This PC also shows the page and keeps its own history in [CommonAppDataFolder]Usage Control." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!--
        The router the hub shows as a device, only with the website on. The
        user and password fields show only for the ways that need a login,
        and the community only for SNMP v2c. Next checks the fields for what
        HUB_ROUTERS cannot hold (RouterInvalid) and stays on the page until
        they are fine; the service checks the rest when it starts.
      -->
      <Dialog Id="RouterDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}Should the hub show your router?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Its traffic and uptime appear as a device of its own." />
        <Control Id="KindLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="52" Width="75" Height="12" Text="&amp;Router:" />
        <Control Id="Kind" Type="ComboBox" X="100" Y="50" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_KIND" ComboList="yes">
          <ComboBox Property="ROUTER_KIND">
            <ListItem Value="none" Text="No router" />
            <ListItem Value="upnp" Text="Any router with UPnP (no login)" />
            <ListItem Value="fritzbox" Text="FRITZ!Box (user and password)" />
            <ListItem Value="asus" Text="ASUS (user and password)" />
            <ListItem Value="snmp" Text="SNMP v2c (community)" />
            <ListItem Value="snmpv3" Text="SNMPv3 (user and password)" />
            <ListItem Value="keep" Text="Keep the routers set before" />
          </ComboBox>
        </Control>
        <Control Id="KeepText" Type="Text" X="100" Y="74" Width="250" Height="24" ShowCondition='ROUTER_KIND = "keep"' HideCondition='ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep"' Text="[HUB_ROUTERS]" />
        <Control Id="AddressLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="74" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' Text="&amp;Address:" />
        <Control Id="Address" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="72" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_ADDRESS" Text="{253}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' />
        <Control Id="NameLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="96" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' Text="&amp;Name on the page:" />
        <Control Id="Name" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="94" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_NAME" Text="{64}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' />
        <Control Id="UserLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="118" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Text="&amp;User:" />
        <Control Id="User" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="116" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_USER" Text="{64}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' />
        <Control Id="PasswordLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="140" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Text="&amp;Password:" />
        <Control Id="CommunityLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="140" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='ROUTER_KIND = "snmp"' HideCondition='ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "snmp"' Text="&amp;Community:" />
        <Control Id="Secret" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="138" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_SECRET" Password="yes" Text="{128}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp")' />
        <Control Id="PasswordHint" Type="Text" X="100" Y="157" Width="250" Height="20" NoPrefix="yes" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Text="Empty keeps the password stored before for this name. SNMPv3 needs at least 8 characters." />
        <Control Id="CommunityHint" Type="Text" X="100" Y="157" Width="250" Height="20" NoPrefix="yes" ShowCondition='ROUTER_KIND = "snmp"' HideCondition='ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "snmp"' Text="Empty for public, or the community stored before for this name." />
        <Control Id="InvalidText" Type="Text" X="20" Y="179" Width="330" Height="12" NoPrefix="yes" ShowCondition="RouterInvalid" HideCondition="NOT RouterInvalid" Text="Fill in the address (no port for UPnP), a name other than Local and the user, without commas, = or @." />
        <Control Id="PrivacyText" Type="Text" X="20" Y="195" Width="330" Height="36" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Read from the router over the local network only. No data is sent to any server; everything stays on your own devices, and a router password is stored encrypted where the system allows it." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <Dialog Id="EditionDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}Which edition should it run?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Den-Watcher runs each add-on as a small program of its own." />
        <Control Id="Edition" Type="RadioButtonGroup" X="20" Y="60" Width="330" Height="110" Property="EDITION">
          <RadioButtonGroup Property="EDITION">
            <RadioButton Value="cub" X="0" Y="0" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Cub: the lightweight add-ons (recommended)" />
            <RadioButton Value="grizzly" X="0" Y="56" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Grizzly: pick what to track, group by group" />
          </RadioButtonGroup>
        </Control>
        <Control Id="CubText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="77" Width="310" Height="36" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Tracks system details (kernel, memory details, pressure) and the network (Wi-Fi quality and the networks nearby), reading a few counters at each poll." />
        <Control Id="GrizzlyText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="133" Width="310" Height="36" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Starts from Cub's choices, and adds power, the graphics card, disk health, listening ports, processes and Bluetooth, which cost more or need more rights." />
        <Control Id="AlwaysText" Type="Text" X="20" Y="200" Width="330" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Both always track CPU, memory, disks, network traffic, temperatures, graphics cards and the battery." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!--
        The Grizzly page: what to track, in the same groups as install.sh,
        Docker's profiles and the Android app. Each group has a box, with the
        boxes of its add-ons below it, and below an add-on what it collects
        (WIFI_NEARBY and WIFI_SCAN, BLUETOOTH_NAMES); a box is greyed out
        while one above it is cleared. Groups of one add-on, Bluetooth and
        Processes, are that add-on's box. Linux-only add-ons (inodes,
        containers) have none. Next turns the boxes into the options.
      -->
      <Dialog Id="AddOnsDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}What should Grizzly track?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Cub's choices are ticked. Point at a box for what it reads." />
        <Control Id="Details" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="52" Width="165" Height="14" Property="DETAILS_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="System &amp;details" ToolTip="Kernel, memory details and pressure" />
        <Control Id="Kernel" Type="CheckBox" X="32" Y="67" Width="153" Height="14" Property="KERNEL_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Kernel" ToolTip="Context switches, interrupts, open handles and TCP connections" EnableCondition='DETAILS_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (DETAILS_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Memory" Type="CheckBox" X="32" Y="82" Width="153" Height="14" Property="MEMORY_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Memory details" ToolTip="Committed memory, pools, page faults and paging" EnableCondition='DETAILS_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (DETAILS_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Pressure" Type="CheckBox" X="32" Y="97" Width="153" Height="14" Property="PRESSURE_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="Press&amp;ure" ToolTip="Threads waiting for the CPU, pages read from disk and busy disks" EnableCondition='DETAILS_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (DETAILS_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Hardware" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="112" Width="165" Height="14" Property="HARDWARE_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Hardware" ToolTip="Power draw, graphics card details and disk health" />
        <Control Id="Power" Type="CheckBox" X="32" Y="127" Width="153" Height="14" Property="POWER_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Power draw" ToolTip="What the CPU, battery and NVIDIA graphics card draw" EnableCondition='HARDWARE_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (HARDWARE_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Gpu" Type="CheckBox" X="32" Y="142" Width="153" Height="14" Property="GPU_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Graphics card details" ToolTip="The fan, clocks, video engines and power limit of NVIDIA cards" EnableCondition='HARDWARE_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (HARDWARE_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Smart" Type="CheckBox" X="32" Y="157" Width="153" Height="14" Property="SMART_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="D&amp;isk health (SMART)" ToolTip="The disks' health, temperature and wear; runs as a service of its own with more rights" EnableCondition='HARDWARE_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (HARDWARE_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Processes" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="172" Width="165" Height="14" Property="PROCESSES_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="P&amp;rocesses" ToolTip="The twenty processes using the most CPU and memory" />
        <Control Id="Network" Type="CheckBox" X="192" Y="52" Width="165" Height="14" Property="NETWORK_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="N&amp;etwork" ToolTip="Wi-Fi and listening ports" />
        <Control Id="Wifi" Type="CheckBox" X="204" Y="67" Width="153" Height="14" Property="WIFI_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Wi-Fi quality and signal" ToolTip="The link quality and signal of each connected Wi-Fi adapter" EnableCondition='NETWORK_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (NETWORK_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="WifiNearby" Type="CheckBox" X="216" Y="82" Width="141" Height="14" Property="WIFI_NEARBY_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="Networks ne&amp;arby" ToolTip="The Wi-Fi networks nearby, each with its signal, channel and security (WIFI_NEARBY)" EnableCondition='NETWORK_BOX AND WIFI_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (NETWORK_BOX AND WIFI_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="WifiScan" Type="CheckBox" X="228" Y="97" Width="129" Height="14" Property="WIFI_SCAN_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Scan every 5 minutes" ToolTip="Asks Windows to scan for them every 5 minutes; a scan can delay the connection for a moment (WIFI_SCAN)" EnableCondition='NETWORK_BOX AND WIFI_BOX AND WIFI_NEARBY_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (NETWORK_BOX AND WIFI_BOX AND WIFI_NEARBY_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Ports" Type="CheckBox" X="204" Y="112" Width="153" Height="14" Property="PORTS_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="Listening p&amp;orts" ToolTip="Which TCP and UDP ports the PC listens on" EnableCondition='NETWORK_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (NETWORK_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="Bluetooth" Type="CheckBox" X="192" Y="127" Width="165" Height="14" Property="BLUETOOTH_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="Blue&amp;tooth" ToolTip="The connected headsets, speakers and other devices" />
        <Control Id="BluetoothNames" Type="CheckBox" X="204" Y="142" Width="153" Height="14" Property="BLUETOOTH_NAMES_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="Devi&amp;ce names" ToolTip="Shows each device by its name, which often carries its owner's; without, by its kind, such as Headphones (BLUETOOTH_NAMES)" EnableCondition='BLUETOOTH_BOX' DisableCondition='NOT (BLUETOOTH_BOX)' />
        <Control Id="AlwaysText" Type="Text" X="20" Y="200" Width="330" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Always tracked: CPU, memory, disks, network traffic, temperatures, graphics cards and the battery." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!--
        The Remove page, after Remove on the maintenance page of an installer
        run again: one box for the history (DELETE_HISTORY) and one for the
        router passwords (DELETE_ROUTER_PASSWORDS). Windows' own Uninstall
        button in Apps runs no page, so it keeps the history and deletes the
        passwords. The page names the data folder from CommonAppDataFolder,
        so it is right where ProgramData was moved.
      -->
      <Dialog Id="RemoveDataDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}What else should go?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="What is not ticked stays in [CommonAppDataFolder]Usage Control." />
        <Control Id="DeleteHistory" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="55" Width="330" Height="15" Property="DELETE_HISTORY" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="Delete the &amp;history" />
        <Control Id="DeleteHistoryText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="72" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Kept, installing Den-Watcher again shows it again." />
        <Control Id="DeleteRouterPasswords" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="100" Width="330" Height="15" Property="DELETE_ROUTER_PASSWORDS" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="Delete the &amp;router passwords" />
        <Control Id="DeleteRouterPasswordsText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="117" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Kept, installing Den-Watcher again with the same router name and an empty password uses them again." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!--
        After an install or update, Finish shows the tray icon when its box is
        ticked. A repair from the wizard closes the icon (see
        CloseApplication), and WiX's last page shows the box only for an
        install, so Finish then shows the icon again without asking.
      -->
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="DoAction" Value="StartTray" Order="998"
               Condition='(WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX = 1 AND NOT Installed) OR WixUI_InstallMode = "Repair"' />
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="EndDialog" Value="Return" Order="999" />

      <Publish Dialog="WelcomeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="ModeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="ModeDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="WelcomeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="ModeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="RouterDlg" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
      <Publish Dialog="ModeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" Condition='WEBSITE &lt;&gt; "1"' />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="ModeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="RouterInvalid" Value="1" Condition='ROUTER_SECRET &gt;&lt; Quote OR ROUTER_SECRET &gt;&lt; "\0" OR (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep") AND (NOT ROUTER_ADDRESS OR NOT ROUTER_NAME OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; " " OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "/" OR (ROUTER_KIND = "upnp" AND ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; ":") OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "\0" OR ROUTER_NAME ~= "Local" OR ((ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3") AND (NOT ROUTER_USER OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; ":" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; " ")))' Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="RouterInvalid" Value="{}" Condition='NOT (ROUTER_SECRET &gt;&lt; Quote OR ROUTER_SECRET &gt;&lt; "\0" OR (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep") AND (NOT ROUTER_ADDRESS OR NOT ROUTER_NAME OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; " " OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "/" OR (ROUTER_KIND = "upnp" AND ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; ":") OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "\0" OR ROUTER_NAME ~= "Local" OR ((ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3") AND (NOT ROUTER_USER OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; ":" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; " "))))' Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="HUB_ROUTERS" Value=" " Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND ROUTER_KIND = "none"' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="HUB_ROUTERS" Value="[ROUTER_NAME]=[ROUTER_KIND]:[ROUTER_ADDRESS]" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND (ROUTER_KIND = "upnp" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp")' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="HUB_ROUTERS" Value="[ROUTER_NAME]=[ROUTER_KIND]:[ROUTER_USER]@[ROUTER_ADDRESS]" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="ROUTER_PASSWORDS" Value="[ROUTER_NAME]=[ROUTER_SECRET]" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp") AND ROUTER_SECRET' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="ROUTER_PASSWORDS" Value="{}" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep" AND NOT ((ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp") AND ROUTER_SECRET)' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" Condition="NOT RouterInvalid" Order="3" />
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="RouterDlg" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="ModeDlg" Condition='WEBSITE &lt;&gt; "1"' />
      <!-- Cub installs its add-ons and none of the others; what they collect stays as it was. -->
      <?foreach AddOn in PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;MEMORY ?>
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="1" Condition='EDITION = "cub"' Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <?foreach AddOn in POWER;GPU;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES;BLUETOOTH ?>
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="0" Condition='EDITION = "cub"' Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" Condition='EDITION = "cub"' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="AddOnsDlg" Condition='EDITION = "grizzly"' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" />
      <!-- An add-on is on when its box and its group's box are ticked. -->
      <?foreach AddOn in KERNEL;MEMORY;PRESSURE ?>
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="1" Condition="DETAILS_BOX AND $(AddOn)_BOX" Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="0" Condition="NOT (DETAILS_BOX AND $(AddOn)_BOX)" Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <?foreach AddOn in POWER;GPU;SMART ?>
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="1" Condition="HARDWARE_BOX AND $(AddOn)_BOX" Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="0" Condition="NOT (HARDWARE_BOX AND $(AddOn)_BOX)" Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <?foreach AddOn in WIFI;PORTS ?>
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="1" Condition="NETWORK_BOX AND $(AddOn)_BOX" Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="0" Condition="NOT (NETWORK_BOX AND $(AddOn)_BOX)" Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <?foreach AddOn in PROCESSES;BLUETOOTH;WIFI_NEARBY;WIFI_SCAN;BLUETOOTH_NAMES ?>
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="1" Condition="$(AddOn)_BOX" Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="0" Condition="NOT $(AddOn)_BOX" Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="AddOnsDlg" Condition='NOT Installed AND EDITION = "grizzly"' />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" Condition='NOT Installed AND EDITION = "cub"' />

      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceWelcomeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="MaintenanceTypeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Control="RepairButton" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Control="RemoveButton" Event="NewDialog" Value="RemoveDataDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="MaintenanceWelcomeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="RemoveDataDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="MaintenanceTypeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="RemoveDataDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Condition='Installed AND WixUI_InstallMode &lt;&gt; "Remove"' />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="RemoveDataDlg" Condition='Installed AND WixUI_InstallMode = "Remove"' />
    </UI>

    <UIRef Id="WixUI_Common" />

    <!-- A ticked box on the wizard's last page shows the tray icon right away. -->
    <SetProperty Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOXTEXT" Value="Show the icon in the taskbar now" After="CostFinalize" Sequence="ui" Condition="NOT Installed" />
    <Property Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX" Value="1" />
    <Property Id="WixShellExecTarget" Value="[#TrayExe]" />
    <CustomAction Id="StartTray" BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_$(sys.BUILDARCHSHORT)" DllEntry="WixShellExec" Impersonate="yes" Return="ignore" />
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
